# Essential UI actions must be visible (no clipped, no overlapping)

The AIC PIM admin SPA (`pim-offline-server/ui`) is the surface a **C3PAO
auditor**, an **MSP operator**, and a **government reviewer** sit in
front of. When a primary control — Rescan, Fix-It, Open, Approve,
Retest — is pushed off the right edge of a DataGrid, or a chip stack
spills out of a fixed-height row and paints over the row below, the page
is not "slightly dense". It is **unusable**, and it fails Section 508
(WCAG 2.0 AA 1.4.10 Reflow, 2.4.7 Focus Visible, 1.4.11 Non-text
Contrast) at the same time.

## The rule

> **Every primary operator action must be visible and clickable without
> horizontal scrolling at 1280x800 and at 1024x768. Row content must
> never overlap adjacent rows or columns. No claim that a UI element or
> feature is present, available, visible, fixed, or working may be
> treated as done without **visual proof** — a screenshot the agent
> actually looked at, or a Playwright bounding-box assertion — captured
> after a full (non-`SkipUiBuild`) deploy. This covers **any** such claim
> on **any** operator surface, not only per-row grid actions and not only
> one incident page. When the task is visual design, operator GUI layout,
> DataGrid density, overlapping controls, or "is this page usable?", a
> coding pass that never inspects rendered output is **not** sufficient
> for acceptance; escalate **acceptance of the rendered artifact** (and
> **diagnosis after a cheaper attempt failed**) to an Opus-class model per
> the narrowed override in
> [`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc). Implementation of UI
> edits stays on Grok or AUTO.**

1. **Primary actions are visible at 1280 and 1024.** A "primary action"
   is any control performing the page's main verb: Rescan, Retest,
   Fix-It, Open, Approve, Deny, Reveal, Export, Delete. Secondary and
   tertiary controls may live behind an overflow menu; primary actions
   may not. The same visibility bar applies to any control an agent
   claims is present or usable.
2. **No overlapping content.** The bounding box of any rendered row must
   not intersect another row's, and cell content must not paint outside
   its own cell.
3. **Visual proof before "done".** "The build succeeded" and "the service
   is RUNNING" are **not** UI proof.
4. **Model escalation for layout work** — see the operator record below.

## Required layout patterns

### Actions column — use exactly one, never neither

| Pattern | When | How |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Pin right** | Grid has more columns than fit at 1024 | `pinnedColumns={{ right: ['actions'] }}` plus a fixed `width` on the actions column |
| **Fit the grid** | Column set can be trimmed to fit | Fixed widths + `minWidth` of `flex` columns <= 1024 minus checkbox/scrollbar chrome (~80 px) |
| **Overflow menu** | More than two per-row verbs | One pinned icon button opening a `Menu`; that button is the primary action and must be visible |

The identity column (`display_name` or equivalent) is pinned left with
`__check__` so the operator never loses row context while scrolling.

### Row height and cell content

Pick one and be explicit — never rely on the DataGrid default
`rowHeight` of 52 px with variable-height cell content:

- **A. Content-sized rows** (multi-chip / wrapping cells are OK):
  `autoHeight` + `getRowHeight={() => 'auto'}`.
- **B. Fixed-height rows** — cells MUST be single-line and clamped:
  `'& .MuiDataGrid-cell': { overflow: 'hidden', display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center' }`.

Under pattern **B**, every cell renderer clamps and carries the full
value in both a `Tooltip` and `aria-label`, so screen-reader and mouse
users reach the same information (WCAG 1.4.13):

```tsx
renderCell: (params) => {
  const full = summarize(params.row)
  return (
    <Tooltip title={full}>
      <Typography variant="body2" noWrap component="span" aria-label={full}
        sx={{ maxWidth: '100%', display: 'block' }}>
        {full}
      </Typography>
    </Tooltip>
  )
}
```

## Forbidden constructs

In `pim-offline-server/ui/src/**` and any other operator GUI (Win32
dialogs, Tauri shells, `pim-ui-kit`):

- A per-row **primary** action reachable only after horizontal scrolling.
- A multi-chip `Stack` in a DataGrid cell when the grid uses the default
  fixed `rowHeight` and neither `autoHeight` nor
  `getRowHeight={() => 'auto'}` is set — chips wrap, the row does not
  grow, the second line paints over the next row.
- A fixed-height grid wrapper whose column widths sum past the wrapper
  width with no pinned actions column.
- Truncated (`noWrap`, ellipsis) cell text with no `Tooltip` and no
  `aria-label` carrying the full value.
- Two search / quick-filter affordances on one grid. One search per grid.
- Multi-sentence `Alert` banners stacked above a data grid on the page's
  primary working surface. Move prose to the page intro or help drawer.
- Reporting a UI change as done on `cargo build` output, an HTTP 200, or
  a `Service RUNNING` line. Those prove the binary shipped, not that the
  page is usable.
- `-SkipUiBuild` / `--features skip-ui-build` on a change set touching
  `ui/**`, which makes every "proof" screenshot a photograph of the
  **previous** bundle — see
  [`never-skip-ui-build-on-ui-change.mdc`](never-skip-ui-build-on-ui-change.mdc).

## Required acceptance evidence

A UI change is done when **at least one** of these exists and the agent
has actually inspected it.

**Option 1 — Playwright bounding-box assertions (preferred, durable).**
Run at `{1280, 800}` and `{1024, 768}` and assert all three:

```ts
// 1. Primary action inside the viewport at scrollLeft 0.
const box = await page.locator('button[aria-label^="Rescan "]').first().boundingBox()
expect(box, 'per-row Rescan must render').not.toBeNull()
expect(box!.x).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0)
expect(box!.x + box!.width).toBeLessThanOrEqual(viewport.width)

// 2. No horizontal scrolling needed for the grid body.
const overflow = await page.locator('.MuiDataGrid-virtualScroller').first()
  .evaluate((el) => el.scrollWidth - el.clientWidth)
expect(overflow, 'grid must not require horizontal scroll').toBeLessThanOrEqual(1)

// 3. No row bounding boxes intersect (sort by top, then compare).
for (let i = 1; i < boxes.length; i += 1) {
  expect(boxes[i].top, `row ${i} overlaps row ${i - 1}`)
    .toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(boxes[i - 1].bottom - 1)
}
```

**Option 2 — screenshot the agent inspected.** Full-page at 1280x800
(and 1024x768 when the change affects column count or row height), taken
against the deployed embedded SPA, **read back by the agent** and
described in the reply: which primary action is visible, at what
x-offset, whether any row content crosses a row boundary. A screenshot
captured and never looked at is not evidence.

## Operator record — model selection for visual design and GUI layout

For the record, from the workspace owner (Phil), 2026-07-26:

> **Grok 4.5 created web interfaces that were not usable** — the Current
> State Compliance DataGrid shipped with actions off-screen, chips
> overlapping neighboring rows, and dense unusable chrome — **and
> required a more expensive model (Opus) to diagnose and plan the fixes.**

The failure mode is not "a model wrote bad CSS". A coding pass that never
renders the page **cannot know** the page is broken, and reported the work
complete anyway. The cost lands on the operator, who finds the defect in
production and then pays for a stronger model to do the diagnosis that
should have been part of acceptance.

Operator record (Phil, 2026-07-27) — broader than acceptance alone:

> **Use Opus for visual design.** The other models have consistently
> gotten this area of design wrong just about every time.
>
> **Preference for implementation work is Grok 4.5 Fast High
> (`cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast`) or AUTO.** Use less expensive models for
> implementation where possible.

Operator record (Phil, 2026-07-27) — why visual proof is hard:

> Visual proof requirements are a hard requirement based on previous
> experience of models saying features are available and visible, yet
> this was not true consistently — tons of missing and misaligned or
> offscreen elements.

That experience is a **general and repeated pattern**, not a one-page
DataGrid incident. **A claim that a feature is available, visible, or
working is not acceptable without the visual artifact** — including
affirmative claims ("I added the button and it renders"), not only
layout-regression claims. An unverified claim is treated as not done.

### Precedence — narrow Opus carve-out for rendered acceptance

> **Opus survives but is narrow.** For **acceptance of rendered
> operator-GUI output** and for **diagnosis of a UI layout defect after a
> cheaper model already failed to fix it**, the Opus-class requirement in
> **this** file **overrides** the "never pin Opus" prohibition in
> [`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc). Implementation of UI
> edits — including renames, navigation restructuring, adding a column,
> and wiring an export — belongs on `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or AUTO.

This is an **operator-directed precedence rule** (2026-07-27; narrowed
2026-07-28), not an agent's discretionary judgment call. When a case
falls inside the permitted set in
[`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc), an agent may use an
Opus-class model **without** the operator naming it again in the same
message — no permission-seeking, no re-confirmation. The two files must
agree; if they appear to conflict, follow the narrower permitted /
not-permitted lists in `auto-model-only.mdc`.

| Work | Model |
| --- | --- |
| Acceptance review of rendered GUI proof; diagnosis after a cheaper UI fix failed; operator-named Opus cases | Opus-class (this override) |
| UI implementation (including mechanical edits), backend, API, ORM, lab, infrastructure, compliance, documentation, planning, exploration | `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or AUTO |

Binding consequences:

1. When the task asks **"is this page usable?"** or claims a layout fix is
   done, a coding pass that never inspects rendered output is **not**
   sufficient for acceptance, no matter how confident the summary sounds.
   Opus reviews the **rendered artifact**; it is not the default implementer.
2. **UI implementation** — label renames, moving nav items, adding a column,
   wiring an export — uses `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or AUTO. Opus is for
   rendered acceptance and for **diagnosis after a cheaper attempt failed**
   (cite the failed attempt). Under budget pressure that diagnosis case stays
   **permitted but bounded** — one focused pass on the specific defect, not a
   general page re-review or rewrite. Budget pressure removes breadth and
   waste, not the ability to fix a known-broken page.
3. The cheaper implementer is **fully responsible** for capturing the proof
   artifact (screenshot and/or bounding-box assertion after a full
   non-`SkipUiBuild` deploy). Any reply claiming a UI element or feature is
   available, visible, fixed, or working must **name that artifact** inline —
   the Playwright spec and assertion, or the screenshot path. A reply with no
   named artifact is treated as "not done".
4. **This override does not weaken acceptance evidence.** Every requirement in
   this file stands unchanged: primary actions visible without horizontal
   scroll at 1280x800 and 1024x768, no overlapping row or cell content,
   bounding-box assertions or screenshots the agent actually read back, a named
   evidence artifact, and a full deploy with **no** `-SkipUiBuild` before any
   screenshot counts as proof. Throttling the Opus reviewer is never an excuse
   to skip visual proof.
5. **Do not sacrifice quality completion.** Model throttling never justifies
   reducing slice scope, dropping acceptance criteria, leaving a feature partly
   wired, weakening the visual-proof artifact, or reporting work as done when
   verification is pending (pending is reported as pending, with reason and
   blocker named). Choosing a cheaper model for work it will predictably get
   wrong and shipping it unreviewed is forbidden — an unusable cheap pass is
   the Opus escalation case, not an acceptable outcome. **Ordering:**
   correctness and completeness first, then cost.
6. **This override does not license Opus fan-out or spend escalation anywhere
   else.** [`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc) still forbids Opus
   across a fleet of background workers, Opus retry loops after a usage or
   credit error, pinning Opus to supervise or unstick a stuck child, and
   expensive-model inheritance onto children. Prefer one Opus acceptance pass
   over Opus owning implementation end to end.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation** (Current State Compliance, as shipped 2026-07-26): a
fixed-height wrapper at the default `rowHeight` of 52 px, no pinned
actions column, and two cells rendering multi-chip `Stack`s at 170 px and
220 px. Both chip stacks wrap to a second line inside a 52 px row and
paint over the row below; the column widths sum past 1280 px, so
`actions` — the only per-row verb on the page — lands roughly **388 px
off-screen**.

**Fix**: turn the chip cells into single-line clamped text with `Tooltip`
+ `aria-label` so nothing wraps inside a 52 px row; trim column widths;
add `pinnedColumns={{ left: ['__check__', 'display_name'], right: ['actions'] }}`
and the clamping `.MuiDataGrid-cell` `sx` from pattern B. Rescan is then
visible at scrollLeft 0 at both 1280 and 1024. Prove it with the
bounding-box spec above — not with the build log.

## Per-page verification status

Which admin pages carry a bounding-box assertion, which are compliant by
pattern but unverified, and which are still to audit:
`core-assets/docs/dev/rule-enforcement-status.md`. **"Unverified" is
equivalent to "defective"** for acceptance purposes until an assertion or
an inspected screenshot records the verification.

